/* Original programming and computer-science lessons for the open library. */
(function() {
  function lesson(title, level, paragraphs, example, practice, question, answer, language, code) {
    return { title, level, minutes: level === "Advanced" ? 9 : level === "Intermediate" ? 7 : 5,
      read: paragraphs, example, practice, question, answer, codeLanguage: language, code };
  }
  const P = (title, description, accent, nodes, references, lessons) => ({
    id: title.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, ""),
    title, category: "PROGRAMMING & COMPUTER SCIENCE", description,
    diagram: { kind: "flow", accent, caption: description, nodes },
    references, lessons
  });
  const L = lesson;
  const paths = [
    P("Java Programming", "Learn Java from the JVM and language basics to object-oriented design, collections, errors, files, and tests.", "#ff8d6e",
      ["SOURCE", "COMPILER", "BYTECODE", "JVM"], [{label:"Dev.java learning materials",url:"https://dev.java/learn/"}], [
      L("How Java code runs","Beginner",["Java is statically typed: the compiler checks many mistakes before a program runs. You write source files ending in .java, compile them into bytecode, and let a Java Virtual Machine execute that bytecode on an operating system.","The JDK is the developer kit and contains tools such as the compiler. The JVM runs bytecode and manages execution and memory. This separation is why the same compiled program can run on different systems with a compatible JVM.","When code fails, identify the stage. A compiler error points to source; a runtime exception happens after startup. Read the first useful error and its file and line number before changing code."],"A console app is compiled to bytecode and run anywhere a compatible Java runtime is installed.","Sketch the route from Main.java to printed output. Label the compiler and JVM.","What does the JVM execute?","Java bytecode; the JDK compiler translates source code into bytecode first.","java","class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(\"Hello, learner!\");\n  }\n}"),
      L("Variables, types, and conversion","Beginner",["A variable names a value. Java asks you to declare a type: int for whole numbers, double for decimal measurements, boolean for true or false, and String for text. The type lets the compiler reject many invalid operations.","Pick a type from the domain. Counts fit integer types, while money calculations may need BigDecimal rather than binary floating-point. Represent missing values explicitly instead of using a magic number that could also be valid data.","Conversions can lose information. Converting a double to an int discards the fractional part. Validate user input at the boundary and make any lossy conversion deliberate."],"A quiz app stores correct answers as an int and a display name as a String.","Declare a book title, page count, and availability flag. Explain each type choice.","Why does Java require declared types?","Types describe valid values and let the compiler catch incompatible operations.","java","String title = \"Clean Code\";\nint pages = 464;\nboolean available = true;"),
      L("Conditions, loops, and methods","Beginner",["Operators compare and combine values; if/else selects a branch. Use parentheses when a condition is complex so the intended logic is obvious to another reader.","Use for when the range is known and while when repetition depends on a condition. Every loop needs progress toward stopping; arrays start at zero, so their last index is length minus one.","A method names a reusable task. Parameters are inputs and a return value is output. Separate calculation from printing so the calculation can be reused and tested without capturing console output."],"A library can calculate overdue days in a method and display the result in a separate screen.","Write a method that returns the larger of two values; include an equal-input case.","What must a loop eventually do?","Make its stopping condition true, or it can run forever.","java","static int larger(int a, int b) {\n  return a > b ? a : b;\n}"),
      L("Arrays, strings, and common mistakes","Beginner",["An array stores a fixed number of values of one type. Index access is fast, but an index outside 0 through length minus one is invalid. Use a collection when the number of items needs to grow.","String is immutable, so edits create a new string. For many repeated edits, StringBuilder avoids making many temporary strings. Text comparison should normally use equals, not ==, which compares object identity.","Trace the first and last loop iteration on paper when working with indexes. Empty input is an important case: do not access element zero until you know an array has at least one element."],"A temperature report can hold seven readings in an array and compute a mean.","Find the maximum in five readings and explain how the loop stays in bounds.","How do you compare Java strings by text?","Use equals or equalsIgnoreCase when case-insensitive comparison is intended.","java","int[] scores = {72, 88, 91};\nString name = \"Asha\";\nSystem.out.println(name.equals(\"Asha\"));"),
      L("Classes, objects, and encapsulation","Intermediate",["A class describes data and operations; an object is one instance. A constructor establishes valid initial state so callers do not need to remember several setup steps.","Encapsulation keeps fields private and exposes operations that protect rules. A bank account should reject an invalid withdrawal itself instead of relying on every caller to remember the balance rule.","Give a class one clear responsibility. When storage, network calls, formatting, and business rules live together, a small change can break unrelated behavior."],"A Book object can protect its page count and expose an isLong method.","Design a Book with private title/pages, a constructor, and a method reporting whether it is long.","Why keep fields private?","So the class can validate changes and protect its invariants.","java","class Book {\n  private final String title;\n  private final int pages;\n  Book(String title, int pages) {\n    this.title = title; this.pages = pages;\n  }\n  boolean isLong() { return pages >= 400; }\n}"),
      L("Interfaces and polymorphism","Intermediate",["An interface names behavior that different classes can provide. Code depending on the interface can work with several implementations without knowing their internals.","Inheritance models a real is-a relationship, but deep inheritance chains tightly couple child and parent classes. Composition often gives a system more flexibility by combining small capabilities.","Polymorphism lets a shared interface call the implementation supplied at runtime. Tests can use a fake sender, while production uses email, without changing the service that sends messages."],"A notification service accepts any Sender implementation.","Create an interface for a shape's area and name two classes that implement it.","What does polymorphism let a caller do?","Use a shared interface while the runtime object supplies specific behavior.","java","interface Sender { void send(String message); }\nclass ConsoleSender implements Sender {\n  public void send(String message) {\n    System.out.println(message);\n  }\n}"),
      L("Exceptions and reliable file handling","Intermediate",["Exceptions represent failures that interrupt normal flow. Catch a specific exception only where the program can recover or explain what to do next; catching everything can hide corrupted state.","Validate user-controlled data near the boundary. Messages for users should be helpful without exposing private paths or tokens; detailed diagnostics belong in protected logs.","Use try-with-resources so files and network resources close even when operations fail. Always decide what happens when an input file is missing or malformed."],"A grade form catches invalid numeric input and asks the learner to enter digits again.","Design a friendly message for a missing file and a separate private diagnostic entry.","Where should an exception be handled?","At a layer that can recover, explain the failure, or add useful context.","java","try {\n  int age = Integer.parseInt(input);\n} catch (NumberFormatException error) {\n  System.out.println(\"Enter a whole number.\");\n}"),
      L("Collections, generics, and streams","Advanced",["List stores ordered items, Set stores unique items, and Map connects keys to values. Choose based on needed operations; HashMap gives average fast lookup but does not promise sorted keys.","Generics such as List<String> let the compiler check element types and avoid manual casts. A TreeMap is appropriate for sorted keys; LinkedHashMap preserves insertion order.","Streams express a filter-transform-collect pipeline. They are not automatically faster than loops: use them when they improve clarity, and avoid hidden side effects that make execution hard to reason about."],"A vocabulary tool maps each term to a definition and filters only active terms.","Keep even numbers, double them, and describe each stage in the pipeline.","What does List<String> communicate?","That the list contains strings, enabling compile-time checks on reads and writes.","java","List<Integer> out = values.stream()\n  .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)\n  .map(n -> n * 2).toList();"),
      L("Build and test a small Java project","Advanced",["A useful first project separates input, domain rules, and output. A study tracker can parse a task, update a collection, and print a summary; each behavior can be tested separately.","Tests should cover normal inputs, boundaries, and failures. Test public behavior rather than private implementation so a refactor does not break a test for no user-visible reason.","Choose a file format and character encoding deliberately. Never commit passwords or tokens to source control. Document how to build and run the project so another learner can reproduce it."],"A tracker saves one task per line and reloads it the next time it starts.","Plan a study tracker with three behaviors, two edge cases, and one test per behavior.","What makes a test useful after refactoring?","It checks externally visible behavior and edge cases rather than internal structure.","java","Path file = Path.of(\"tasks.txt\");\nFiles.writeString(file, \"Read Java\\nPractice loops\\n\");")
    ]),
    P("Python Programming", "Learn Python from readable syntax to collections, functions, files, generators, tests, and small projects.", "#55d8a8",
      ["INPUT", "PYTHON", "DATA", "OUTPUT"], [{label:"Official Python tutorial",url:"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"}], [
      L("Setup, interpreter, and first script","Beginner",["Python is an interpreted language with readable syntax. The interpreter executes instructions in order; a traceback reports the exception type, message, and call locations when something goes wrong.","Use a project folder and virtual environment so packages for one project do not interfere with another. Record dependencies and prefer trusted package sources over commands copied from unknown pages.","Read a traceback from its final lines for the error, then follow the listed file and line numbers to find the cause. Fix one underlying issue at a time."],"A first script greets a learner and prints a simple calculation.","Create a script that prints a greeting and a result on separate lines.","What does the interpreter do?","It reads and executes the program's Python instructions.","python","name = \"Asha\"\nprint(f\"Welcome, {name}!\")\nprint(3 * 7)"),
      L("Names, data types, and type hints","Beginner",["A name refers to a value. Python infers the type at runtime, but programmers still need to distinguish integers, floats, strings, booleans, lists, dictionaries, and None.","Lists can change; tuples cannot. Dictionaries map keys to values and sets hold unique values. The right data structure makes intent clear and avoids workarounds.","Type hints document expected values even though Python does not enforce most hints at runtime. Treat data from forms, files, or networks as untrusted until it is validated and converted."],"A quiz stores scores in a list and learner details in a dictionary.","Store a name, score list, and completion flag. Hint a function that averages scores.","What is the difference between a list and tuple?","Lists are mutable; tuples are immutable sequences.","python","name: str = \"Asha\"\nscores: list[int] = [82, 91, 87]\ncomplete: bool = True"),
      L("Conditions, loops, and iteration","Beginner",["if chooses a path, while repeats until a condition changes, and for iterates over an iterable. Python indentation marks blocks, so consistent formatting is part of program structure.","Iterating over a collection directly is clearer than managing an index when the position is not needed. Empty collections and boundary values should be considered when deciding what a loop does.","break and continue are useful but can obscure a flow when overused. Give loops clear stopping conditions and keep each repeated action small enough to understand."],"A study app loops through due lessons and prints those not yet complete.","Count scores above a passing threshold and also consider the empty-list case.","Why is direct iteration often clearer than indexing?","It avoids manual index management and says you want each item in the collection.","python","passed = 0\nfor score in [72, 48, 91, 65]:\n  if score >= 60:\n    passed += 1\nprint(passed)"),
      L("Functions and reusable logic","Beginner",["A function packages a named task. Parameters are inputs and return values are outputs, which gives the function a natural boundary for reuse, documentation, and tests.","Avoid mutable default arguments such as an empty list because Python evaluates a default once. Use None and create a fresh value inside the function when needed.","Separate calculation from printing and file access. A pure function is easier to test because the same input produces the same result without hidden side effects."],"A grade function returns a label while a separate part of the app displays it.","Write a function returning the larger of two values and test equal inputs.","Why are return values useful?","Callers can reuse and test the result without tying the function to console output.","python","def grade_label(score: int) -> str:\n  if score >= 80:\n    return \"strong\"\n  return \"keep practicing\""),
      L("Lists, dictionaries, sets, and comprehensions","Intermediate",["Lists preserve order, tuples suit fixed groupings, dictionaries provide key lookups, and sets prevent duplicates. A set is useful for membership; a dictionary is useful for finding a record by ID.","Comprehensions create a transformed collection compactly. Use one for a simple mapping or filter; nested conditions and side effects are clearer in ordinary loops.","Generators yield values as needed rather than building the full list. They save memory for large inputs but are generally consumed only once."],"A book catalog uses a dictionary keyed by ISBN and a set of unique genres.","Model a catalog and filter its records to those with more than 100 pages.","Which structure represents an ID-to-record lookup?","A dictionary maps each key to its corresponding value.","python","book = {\"title\": \"Small Steps\", \"pages\": 120}\ntags = {\"python\", \"beginner\", \"python\"}\nlong_books = [book for _ in tags if book[\"pages\"] > 100]"),
      L("Modules, packages, and environments","Intermediate",["A module is a Python file that groups related code. Packages group modules under a namespace. Keep project code separate from third-party dependencies so imports remain understandable.","A virtual environment isolates packages for one project. A dependency file helps another machine recreate the environment; exact versions may be pinned when reproducibility matters.","Do not name your own file after a standard-library module such as json.py. Python could import your file instead, creating confusing errors that look unrelated to your code."],"A flashcard app can put card logic and command-line input in separate modules.","Sketch two modules for a flashcard project and list its outside dependencies.","Why use a virtual environment?","It isolates project packages from other projects and the system installation.","python","from pathlib import Path\nproject_data = Path(\"data\")\nproject_data.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nprint(project_data.resolve())"),
      L("Exceptions, files, and JSON","Intermediate",["Catch a specific exception where the program can recover or explain the next step. A broad catch that silently ignores every problem can turn visible errors into incorrect data.","Use context managers to close files reliably, specify a text encoding, and handle missing or malformed content. JSON is a data format, not proof that its fields are valid.","Validate parsed values before using them, especially when they came from a user or a remote service. Keep helpful user messages separate from detailed private diagnostics."],"A planner saves tasks as JSON and explains when its file cannot be read.","Design an error message for malformed score data that does not reveal private file paths.","What does with help guarantee?","It runs cleanup, such as closing a file, even when an error occurs.","python","import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"tasks.json\")\np.write_text(json.dumps({\"tasks\": [\"read\"]}), encoding=\"utf-8\")"),
      L("Classes, generators, tests, and a mini project","Advanced",["Use a class when data and behavior form one concept with rules to protect. Keep the public interface small; conventions such as a leading underscore communicate internal details but do not enforce access control.","A generator with yield produces one value at a time. This suits large files and streams because the program need not keep every processed value in memory.","Tests should include ordinary behavior, empty inputs, boundaries, and expected errors. A flashcard tool is a good project: define its data, file format, quiz behavior, and tests before adding a user interface."],"A log reader yields one parsed record at a time, and tests check valid and malformed lines.","Plan a flashcard project with one class, one generator, and three tests.","Why can a generator help with a large file?","It yields incrementally instead of storing all processed records at once.","python","def initials(words):\n  for word in words:\n    if word:\n      yield word[0].lower()")
    ]),
    P("C++ Programming", "Learn modern C++ with types, functions, classes, standard containers, ownership, and safer project practices.", "#a69cff",
      ["SOURCE", "COMPILER", "LINKER", "PROGRAM"], [{label:"C++ reference",url:"https://en.cppreference.com/w/"}], [
      L("Compilation and the C++ program","Beginner",["C++ is compiled into native instructions for a target platform. A compiler checks source files and produces object files; a linker combines them with libraries into an executable.","Choose a language standard intentionally and enable compiler warnings. Warnings reveal suspicious conversions and unused values before they become difficult runtime defects.","Compilation errors can cascade from one missing delimiter or type. Fix the first meaningful diagnostic, rebuild, then continue rather than editing many unrelated lines."],"A tiny command-line program compiles into an executable for the current system.","Draw the compiler and linker stages and name one kind of error each can detect.","What does the linker do?","It combines compiled pieces and libraries into an executable.","cpp","#include <iostream>\nint main() {\n  std::cout << \"Hello!\\n\";\n}"),
      L("Types, references, and const","Beginner",["C++ types determine representation and allowed operations. Signed and unsigned integers behave differently around negative values; mixing them can produce surprising comparisons and conversions.","A reference names an existing object; a pointer stores an address that may be absent or changed. const expresses that a function will not modify a value through that view.","Prefer standard types such as std::string and std::vector over raw arrays for everyday tasks. They manage size and storage and reduce manual bookkeeping."],"A search function accepts a const vector reference to avoid copying a large list.","Choose pass-by-value or const-reference for a large read-only vector and explain why.","What does const communicate?","That the value should not be modified through that view.","cpp","void show(const std::string& name) {\n  std::cout << name << '\\n';\n}"),
      L("Functions, scope, and control flow","Beginner",["Functions name operations and declare inputs and outputs. Keep local variables in the narrowest useful scope, which limits hidden state and accidental reuse.","if/else expresses decisions; range-based for loops visit each element without managing indexes. When indexes are needed, verify the bounds, especially for empty containers.","C++ container sizes use unsigned types; mixing them with negative signed values can cause unexpected conversions. Prefer clear checks and standard algorithms over clever arithmetic."],"A report loops over scores and counts those above a threshold.","Trace an empty vector through a loop and explain why accessing its first element is invalid.","What should be checked before reading the first vector item?","That the vector is not empty.","cpp","for (int score : scores) {\n  if (score >= 60) std::cout << score << '\\n';\n}"),
      L("Classes, constructors, and RAII","Intermediate",["A class groups state and operations; its constructor establishes valid initial state. Private fields and narrow public methods let the class protect its invariants.","C++ uses RAII: a resource's lifetime follows an object's lifetime. Standard library types automatically clean up many resources, reducing the chance of leaks on early returns or exceptions.","Use initialization lists and const methods where appropriate. A class should have one clear responsibility rather than mixing storage, presentation, networking, and business rules."],"A file wrapper can own its resource and close it automatically when its object leaves scope.","Design a class that prevents a page count below one and state where it validates input.","What does RAII connect?","Resource acquisition and cleanup to an object's lifetime.","cpp","class Counter {\n  int value;\npublic:\n  explicit Counter(int start) : value(start) {}\n  int current() const { return value; }\n};"),
      L("STL containers, algorithms, and iterators","Intermediate",["vector is a strong default for sequential data; map keeps keys sorted; unordered_map supports average fast lookup. Choose based on actual operations, not on a vague idea that one container is always best.","Algorithms such as sort and find_if express familiar work and reduce custom loops. Still understand their ordering rules and how insertions or removals may invalidate iterators.","A vector may reallocate when it grows, invalidating pointers and references to its elements. Read the operation's contract before keeping references across updates."],"A contact app can use a vector for display order and a map for lookup by ID.","Choose containers for an ordered ranked list and for fast lookup by unique ID.","Why use a standard algorithm?","It offers familiar reusable behavior and reduces custom code to maintain.","cpp","std::vector<int> scores{82, 41, 95};\nstd::sort(scores.begin(), scores.end());"),
      L("Ownership and smart pointers","Advanced",["A raw pointer does not explain who owns an object. Modern C++ uses values and smart pointers to make lifetime intent clearer: unique_ptr for one owner and shared_ptr for shared lifetime.","Prefer values and references when they fit. Heap allocation adds lifetime questions and is not automatically better; use unique ownership when dynamic lifetime is actually needed.","Shared pointers can form cycles that never release memory. Draw ownership arrows and distinguish an owning edge from a non-owning observation."],"A tree usually owns its children, so exclusive ownership may fit better than shared ownership.","Draw ownership arrows for a parent and two children and mark each edge.","Which pointer suits one clear owner?","std::unique_ptr.","cpp","auto lesson = std::make_unique<std::string>(\"Ownership\");\nstd::cout << *lesson;"),
      L("Templates, errors, and tested projects","Advanced",["Templates let one algorithm work with several types while keeping compile-time checks. Use them when behavior is genuinely shared; unnecessary abstraction can make errors harder to understand.","Expected absence and failures need a clear contract, whether represented by a return type or an exception. The caller should be able to understand what can fail and what to do next.","Build a small score analyzer in steps: parse input, validate it, compute a result, then test empty, duplicate, and invalid data. Document the compiler and steps needed to reproduce it."],"A score analyzer sorts values and computes a median while reporting invalid input safely.","Plan three tests for a score analyzer, including an empty list.","Why test edge cases?","They expose assumptions about empty, invalid, smallest, or repeated values.","cpp","template <typename T>\nT larger(T a, T b) { return a < b ? b : a; }")
    ]),
    P("Web Development", "Build accessible pages with HTML, CSS, JavaScript, browser APIs, HTTP, forms, and secure publishing.", "#ffc66d",
      ["HTML", "CSS", "JAVASCRIPT", "BROWSER"], [{label:"MDN Learn web development",url:"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"}], [
      L("How the browser loads a page","Beginner",["A browser requests a URL, receives a response, and interprets HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, and other resources to build a page. HTTP defines request and response messages; status codes summarize the outcome.","HTTPS adds encryption in transit and server authentication through TLS. It does not make every application action safe by itself, so authorization and input validation remain important.","A static site can be interactive with search and local progress. Shared accounts and private synchronized data need a trusted backend with access rules."],"A learner opens a lesson URL; the browser downloads files and renders its layout.","Draw a request and response, including the URL, status code, and body.","What does HTTPS add?","Encryption in transit and server authentication through TLS.","http","GET /lessons HTTP/1.1\nHost: academy.example\nAccept: text/html"),
      L("Semantic HTML and accessibility","Beginner",["HTML describes meaning and structure. Use headings in order, buttons for actions, links for navigation, and landmarks such as main and nav to help users and assistive technology understand a page.","A clickable div may look like a button but miss keyboard support and expected behavior. Native elements come with semantics and interaction rules built in.","Every form field needs a label. Informative images need useful alternative text; decorative images should not create redundant announcements."],"An article uses header, nav, main, article, and footer to explain the page structure.","Sketch a lesson page with topic navigation, article, and footer.","Why choose a button instead of a clickable div?","A button supplies built-in keyboard, focus, and action semantics.","html","<main>\n  <article>\n    <h1>Arrays in Java</h1>\n    <p>Store related values together.</p>\n    <button type=\"button\">Mark as read</button>\n  </article>\n</main>"),
      L("CSS layout and responsive design","Beginner",["CSS controls presentation. Flexbox arranges items along one axis; Grid handles rows and columns; normal document flow is often the simplest choice for article text.","Responsive design adapts to available space instead of assuming one screen size. Use flexible widths, readable line lengths, and media queries when the content needs a layout change.","Design tokens centralize colors, spacing, and typography. Consistent focus and contrast choices improve usability across the whole site."],"A course catalog uses three columns on a large screen and one column on a phone.","Sketch a desktop and phone card layout and choose the point where it should change.","When should you add a media query?","When a viewport range needs a meaningful layout adjustment.","css",".cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }\n@media (max-width: 700px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }"),
      L("JavaScript values and flow","Beginner",["JavaScript runs in browsers and server runtimes. const prevents reassignment of a binding; let allows it. Prefer const by default to reduce accidental changes.","Objects and arrays can still be mutated through const; the binding is fixed, not deeply immutable. Keep that distinction in mind when sharing data between parts of a page.","Validate form, URL, and network values because they may be missing or malformed. Use strict comparisons and explicit empty states instead of assuming ideal input."],"A topic search trims whitespace, ignores case, and filters lesson titles.","Write pseudocode for an empty search and a search with no matches.","What does const prevent?","Reassignment of the binding; it does not freeze referenced objects.","javascript","const query = \" Arrays \".trim().toLowerCase();\nconst matches = titles.filter(t => t.toLowerCase().includes(query));"),
      L("DOM events, forms, and input safety","Intermediate",["The DOM represents a page as nodes JavaScript can read and update. Event listeners respond to actions such as typing or clicking; keep handlers focused and provide visible keyboard focus.","Labels and clear error text help people understand form fields. Browser-side validation improves feedback but cannot be trusted as a security boundary because it can be bypassed.","Avoid inserting untrusted strings as HTML. Use textContent to display text safely and validate data again on the server before making important changes."],"A search field listens for input and updates visible lessons without turning the typed query into markup.","Design clear behavior for empty input, invalid input, and no matching lessons.","Is browser validation enough to secure submitted data?","No. The server must validate all incoming data independently.","javascript","const status = document.querySelector(\"#status\");\nsearch.addEventListener(\"input\", () => {\n  status.textContent = search.value;\n});"),
      L("Fetch, JSON, and API states","Intermediate",["An API is a documented interface for exchanging data. fetch can request JSON, but code should check unsuccessful status codes and handle network failures before trusting the result.","JSON represents values but does not guarantee required fields exist. Validate its shape before use, and represent loading, success, empty, and error states distinctly in the interface.","Never put a private server key in public browser code. A public client key is safe only when its service is designed for exposure and server-side policies restrict what it can do."],"A catalog API returns lesson metadata; the browser confirms it received an array before displaying items.","List the UI states needed when loading a course catalog from a network.","Why check response.ok?","A completed HTTP response can still represent an error status.","javascript","const response = await fetch(\"/topics.json\");\nif (!response.ok) throw new Error(\"Load failed\");\nconst data = await response.json();"),
      L("Storage, modules, and privacy","Intermediate",["localStorage persists small strings on one browser profile and is useful for preferences or local progress; it is not a secure vault and does not sync across devices.","ES modules make dependencies explicit through imports and exports. Keep lesson data, rendering, and storage separate so one can change without rewriting the others.","Do not store passwords or long-lived secret tokens in localStorage. Explain what stays local, how it can be cleared, and whether an account is needed for cross-device progress."],"A lesson completion marker can stay local while account progress is stored by a protected backend.","Decide where a theme preference, password, and synchronized progress belong.","Is localStorage suitable for passwords?","No. Page scripts can read it, so it is not a secure credential store.","javascript","const key = \"lesson-progress\";\nlocalStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify({ read: [\"arrays\"] }));"),
      L("Web security and deployment","Advanced",["Escape untrusted output, restrict cross-origin access deliberately, and avoid exposing private keys in public bundles. Authentication and authorization must be enforced by trusted server-side code or database policies.","Publish over HTTPS and review deployment settings. A content security policy and secure headers can reduce some risks, but do not replace careful application logic.","A strong project has accessible controls, useful errors, a clear README, and a rollback route. Measure the experience on a phone and keep dependencies maintainable."],"A public lesson library can be hosted statically while private account features use a protected API.","Make a checklist with security, accessibility, and rollback steps.","Where should a secret API key live?","On a trusted server or secret manager, never in public browser code.","text","untrusted text -> validate -> render as text")
    ]),
    P("SQL & Database Systems", "Learn relational design, SQL queries, joins, constraints, transactions, indexes, migrations, and secure access.", "#70b8ff",
      ["TABLES", "KEYS", "QUERY", "RESULT"], [{label:"PostgreSQL tutorial",url:"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"}], [
      L("Tables, rows, keys, and relationships","Beginner",["A relational database stores records in tables. Each row is one record; each column has a defined type and meaning. A primary key provides a stable identity for a row.","A foreign key connects tables and can prevent references to nonexistent records. Constraints keep rules close to the data so every application benefits from them.","Normalization gives each fact one authoritative home. Copying a learner's email into every lesson record creates update problems when the email changes."],"Learners, courses, and enrollments form three related tables linked by identifiers.","Draw tables for books and authors and mark primary and foreign keys.","What does a primary key do?","Uniquely identifies a row so it can be reliably referenced.","sql","CREATE TABLE learners (\n id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n name TEXT NOT NULL,\n email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL\n);"),
      L("SELECT, WHERE, sorting, and NULL","Beginner",["SELECT reads chosen columns, WHERE filters rows, ORDER BY sorts the results, and LIMIT caps the returned records. Request only the columns the page needs rather than exposing all fields with SELECT *.","NULL means missing or unknown and does not compare like an ordinary value. Use IS NULL when looking for it, and decide whether missing data is allowed in the schema.","A query should reflect the question and the intended sort. Always consider ties when paginating so results do not unexpectedly jump between pages."],"A catalog query returns published lesson titles ordered by their latest update.","Write a query for the ten newest published lessons.","How do you find a missing value?","Use IS NULL because NULL is not equal to an ordinary value.","sql","SELECT title, updated_at FROM lessons\nWHERE published = TRUE\nORDER BY updated_at DESC LIMIT 10;"),
      L("Insert, update, constraints, and safe parameters","Beginner",["INSERT adds rows, UPDATE changes matches, and DELETE removes rows. Before changing production data, inspect which records the condition affects.","NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, and foreign-key constraints encode valid states. They protect data even when several application components write to the same database.","Parameterized queries keep user values separate from SQL syntax. Concatenating user text into a query can let an attacker change the command's meaning."],"A course editor updates one lesson by ID and relies on a unique slug constraint.","Plan how to preview and verify one row before and after an update.","Why use a parameterized query?","The database treats supplied values as data instead of executable SQL syntax.","sql","UPDATE lessons SET title = $1 WHERE id = $2;"),
      L("Joins and relationship cardinality","Intermediate",["A join combines rows. INNER JOIN keeps matches; LEFT JOIN preserves every left-side row and fills unmatched columns with NULL.","Join on intended keys. Joining on a non-unique name can multiply rows and produce totals that look plausible but are wrong.","Reason about whether the relationship is one-to-one, one-to-many, or many-to-many before aggregating. That predicts whether each source row will appear more than once."],"A learner report joins learners to enrollments and then courses.","Explain how a course with zero learners appears in a left join.","What does LEFT JOIN preserve?","Every left-table row, even when it has no match on the right.","sql","SELECT learners.name, courses.title\nFROM learners JOIN enrollments ON enrollments.learner_id = learners.id\nJOIN courses ON courses.id = enrollments.course_id;"),
      L("Grouping and aggregate queries","Intermediate",["COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX summarize records. GROUP BY creates one output per grouping key; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.","COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) ignores NULL values in that column. Know the difference before interpreting a dashboard.","Joins can duplicate rows before aggregation. Validate totals against a smaller query and compare row counts after each join."],"A dashboard counts lessons per path and shows only paths with enough topics.","Calculate average quiz score per learner and filter out groups with few attempts.","When should HAVING be used?","To filter groups based on an aggregate after GROUP BY.","sql","SELECT course_id, COUNT(*) AS total\nFROM lessons GROUP BY course_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 5;"),
      L("Indexes and query plans","Intermediate",["An index helps the database find rows without scanning the entire table. It speeds some reads but uses storage and adds work to inserts, deletes, and updates.","Indexes should match real query patterns. Column order matters in a composite index, and an index for one filter may not help a different query.","Use query plans and representative data to measure. Tiny development tables can hide the true cost of a production query."],"An index on a frequently searched course slug speeds up course lookup as the catalog grows.","Name one query that benefits from an index and one write workload that could be slowed.","Why not index every column?","Indexes cost space and make writes more expensive, and many will not help actual queries.","sql","CREATE INDEX lessons_course_id_idx\nON lessons(course_id);"),
      L("Transactions and consistency","Advanced",["A transaction groups related changes so they succeed together or fail together. This prevents half-finished operations such as debiting one account without crediting another.","ACID describes atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. Isolation choices affect concurrent work; applications should understand defaults and retry behavior.","Keep transactions short. Long transactions hold locks and can block other users or increase conflicts."],"Transferring a balance debits and credits accounts in one transaction.","List the writes in an enrollment transaction and explain what happens if one fails.","What is atomicity?","All changes in a transaction happen together or none are committed.","sql","BEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;"),
      L("Migrations, access control, and backups","Advanced",["A migration is a versioned schema change. Safe changes often add a compatible field first, deploy code that can use it, backfill records, and remove old structures only later.","Database roles should have only needed permissions. Public browser code must never receive an administrator credential; authorization belongs in a trusted service or carefully configured row-level policy.","Backups need retention and restore practice. Logs should not contain passwords, session tokens, or unnecessary personal information."],"Public lessons can be readable while each learner's saved progress remains private to that learner.","Identify which key must remain server-only in a client/database application.","Can a hidden frontend button protect database access?","No. Access rules must be enforced by the server or database policy.","sql","SELECT id, title FROM lessons\nWHERE published = TRUE AND course_id = $1;")
    ]),
    P("Core Computer Science", "Understand operating systems, memory, networks, concurrency, compilers, software tests, and system design.", "#7fe0c0",
      ["APP", "OS", "CPU + MEMORY", "NETWORK"], [{label:"MIT OpenCourseWare",url:"https://ocw.mit.edu/"}], [
      L("What happens when a program runs","Beginner",["A computer follows machine instructions operating on data. The operating system loads a program, grants it resources, and schedules its work beside other programs.","The CPU executes instructions, memory holds active state, and storage keeps data after power is removed. They have different speeds and persistence, so programs move data between them.","A program can compile and still crash: compilation checks one set of rules, while execution depends on real inputs, available resources, and runtime state."],"A browser uses CPU time for scripts, memory for tabs, and storage for cache and settings.","Trace one app action and label CPU, memory, storage, and network.","Which resource keeps data after shutdown?","Persistent storage, such as an SSD or hard drive.","text","input -> instructions -> CPU + memory -> output"),
      L("Operating systems and processes","Beginner",["An operating system manages processes, memory, files, devices, and permissions. A process is a running program with its own address space; a thread is a unit of work within a process.","The scheduler shares CPU time among runnable tasks. Waiting for disk or network input lets the operating system run something else rather than leave the processor idle.","Process isolation limits how apps affect one another. Least privilege reduces damage, but software still needs updates and careful access settings."],"A music player and browser run as separate processes on one computer.","Explain why one frozen app can sometimes close without shutting down the whole computer.","What does a process isolate?","A program's execution state and memory from other processes.","text","program -> process -> scheduled CPU time"),
      L("Memory, stack, and heap","Intermediate",["The call stack tracks active function calls and local variables. The heap supports objects whose lifetime or size is not tied to one function call.","Deep recursion can overflow the stack. A memory leak occurs when a program keeps data it no longer needs, preventing that memory from being reclaimed or reused.","Garbage collection automates many object lifetimes, while languages such as C++ use explicit ownership patterns. Automatic management reduces some errors but not the need to design lifetimes."],"A recursive tree walk uses stack frames; a large collection may occupy heap memory.","Draw three nested function calls and mark which returns first.","What is a memory leak?","Memory remains allocated or referenced after the program no longer needs it.","text","call A -> call B -> call C\nreturn C -> return B -> return A"),
      L("CPU, cache, and binary data","Intermediate",["Caches keep recently used data close to the processor. Accessing nearby values can be faster than jumping around memory, even when both approaches perform a similar number of operations.","Numbers and text are represented as bits; the type and encoding determine how a bit pattern is interpreted. File formats and protocols must define this interpretation.","Overflow occurs when a value exceeds a fixed-width type. Validate sizes and conversions, particularly when inputs come from outside the program."],"Reading a large array in order often uses cache more effectively than random jumps.","Explain in plain language why nearby values can be quicker to read.","What does character encoding define?","How text characters are represented as bytes and decoded into text.","text","bits -> bytes -> typed value -> program meaning"),
      L("Networks, DNS, HTTPS, and HTTP","Intermediate",["Networks move packets between endpoints. IP handles addressing and routing; transport protocols define delivery behavior; application protocols define messages such as HTTP requests.","DNS helps find a service from a human-readable name. TLS protects communication and authenticates a server, but it does not make every application action safe.","Status codes summarize HTTP outcomes. Clients must handle timeouts carefully because retrying a request can repeat an action unless it was designed to be idempotent."],"A browser resolves a site, establishes a protected connection, then requests a page.","Map the DNS, connection, request, and response stages when opening a site.","Why can blindly retrying a payment be dangerous?","The same operation may be performed twice unless it is designed to be idempotent.","text","domain -> DNS -> TLS -> HTTP request -> response"),
      L("Concurrency, threads, and race conditions","Advanced",["Concurrency lets tasks make progress during overlapping time; parallelism runs work at the same moment on multiple processors. A single CPU can still schedule concurrent tasks.","Shared mutable state creates race conditions when task order changes the result. Locks, message passing, immutable data, and ownership rules can coordinate updates.","Threads do not guarantee speed. Work may wait on I/O, be limited by one CPU-bound section, or spend time synchronizing. Measure the bottleneck before adding concurrency."],"A downloader fetches several files concurrently but protects shared progress state.","Find a shared counter in a threaded program and describe one safe update strategy.","What creates a race condition?","A result depends on unpredictable ordering of concurrent access to shared state.","text","task A -- read / update shared value\ntask B -- read / update shared value"),
      L("Compilers, Git, tests, and debugging","Intermediate",["Compilers translate source into another representation or machine instructions; runtimes execute programs. Static checks catch some errors early, but no successful compile proves that logic is correct.","Version control records changes so they can be reviewed and restored. Small focused commits make it easier to understand which change affected behavior.","Debug with evidence: reproduce the failure, narrow the cause, inspect boundaries, and change one thing at a time. Tests should cover public behavior, normal inputs, and edge cases."],"A lesson search issue can be traced from loaded data through filtering to rendering.","Write a three-step bug report for a button that never marks a lesson read.","What makes a debugging hypothesis useful?","It predicts checkable evidence and helps rule possible causes in or out.","text","reproduce -> isolate -> fix -> review change"),
      L("System design and reliability","Advanced",["System design starts with users and constraints: traffic, privacy, latency, availability, and expected failure. A design cannot be evaluated until those needs are explicit.","Caches, queues, databases, and multiple servers add cost and operational work. Start with the simplest architecture that satisfies current needs and scale when evidence calls for it.","Reliability includes backups, monitoring, graceful failures, and recovery plans. A system needs ways to notice problems and restore service, not only a successful first launch."],"A public lesson site can serve static content from a CDN while account progress uses a protected API.","Design a small lesson service and name one failure plus a recovery step.","Why list requirements before choosing services?","They determine what the system must do and which trade-offs matter.","text","requirements -> simple design -> measure -> improve")
    ]),
    P("AI & Data Science", "Learn data preparation, statistics, Python analysis, model evaluation, and responsible AI practice.", "#e7a1ff",
      ["QUESTION", "DATA", "MODEL", "EVALUATE"], [{label:"scikit-learn user guide",url:"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"}], [
      L("Start with a question and a decision","Beginner",["Data science combines domain knowledge, statistics, programming, and communication. Begin by identifying who will use the answer and what decision it should support.","A metric must match the goal. Predicting whether a learner needs help differs from ranking lesson popularity; a convenient score can still reward the wrong outcome.","Write down assumptions and possible harms before collecting information. A simple table or chart may answer the question better than a model."],"A course team examines lesson drop-off to improve explanations rather than label learners as failures.","Turn a vague engagement question into a measurable question and safe action.","Why define a decision before selecting a model?","It clarifies what evidence and evaluation measure are useful.","text","question -> evidence -> decision -> review"),
      L("Collecting and cleaning datasets","Beginner",["A dataset contains observations and fields. Each row needs a clear unit, such as one transaction or learner session, and every column needs a documented meaning.","Cleaning finds missing values, inconsistent units, duplicates, and invalid records. Do not remove inconvenient rows automatically; investigate why they are missing and whether their absence skews results.","Keep raw data unchanged and create repeatable transformations. Record the source, date, permission, and limitations so another person can understand the analysis."],"Feedback data may contain duplicates, empty ratings, and timestamps from different time zones.","List three checks before computing an average survey score.","Why preserve raw data?","It lets the cleaning process be reproduced and corrected without losing evidence.","python","import pandas as pd\ndata = pd.read_csv(\"feedback.csv\")\nprint(data.isna().sum())"),
      L("Statistics, probability, and visualization","Intermediate",["Mean summarizes an average; median is the middle value and resists the influence of extreme observations. Quartiles and range show spread that one average hides.","Choose a visualization to fit the question: histogram for distributions, line chart for ordered time, and bar chart for category comparisons. Axes, units, and sample size should be clear.","Probability describes uncertainty under assumptions, not a guarantee for one case. A large biased sample can still mislead about a wider population."],"Completion time may be skewed when learners leave a tab open, so median can describe a typical session better.","Choose a chart and summary measure for weekly readings across four subjects.","When can median be more useful than mean?","When a small number of extreme values distort the arithmetic average.","python","import statistics\nminutes = [5, 6, 7, 8, 90]\nprint(statistics.median(minutes))"),
      L("Features, labels, and data leakage","Intermediate",["Supervised learning uses examples with labels to learn a mapping from features to a target. Each feature should be available at the time a real prediction is made.","Data leakage occurs when training uses information that would not exist at prediction time. It creates impressive offline scores and poor real-world results.","Split data before fitting transformations that learn from it. A chronological split can better represent future predictions than a random split for time-based problems."],"A model predicting lesson difficulty cannot use a future exam result as an input feature.","Identify one feature that would leak future information into a lesson-completion prediction.","What is a label?","The target outcome that a supervised model is trained to predict.","text","features + known label -> training examples"),
      L("Classification, regression, and evaluation","Advanced",["Classification predicts a category; regression predicts a number. Accuracy can hide weak performance on rare cases, so precision, recall, and a confusion matrix may be more useful.","A threshold turns probability into a decision. Changing it affects false positives and false negatives, so the impact of each error should guide the choice.","Evaluate on data not used for fitting. Compare with a simple baseline, inspect meaningful groups, and state what the result cannot establish."],"A fraud detector may prioritize recall, then route uncertain cases to a human review.","Choose whether precision or recall matters more for a low-risk recommendation.","What does recall measure?","The fraction of actual positive cases the system correctly identifies.","text","confusion matrix -> precision / recall / errors"),
      L("Overfitting, bias, and responsible AI","Advanced",["Overfitting means a model memorizes training patterns that do not generalize. Validation data helps compare designs; a final test set should estimate performance only after choices are made.","Bias can enter through historical labels, missing groups, measurement, and deployment. Metrics alone cannot decide whether an application is fair or appropriate.","Document intended use, data limits, known failure modes, and human oversight. Do not use predictions for high-impact decisions without accountability and suitable review."],"A study recommendation should help learners discover material, not silently deny support based on a questionable prediction.","Write three limits you would disclose before releasing a student-facing model.","Why avoid repeatedly tuning against the final test set?","That leaks information and makes its final performance estimate unreliable.","text","train -> validate choices -> test -> monitor")
    ]),
    P("DevOps & Cloud", "Connect software changes to dependable releases with Git, Linux, containers, CI/CD, monitoring, and backups.", "#ff9fba",
      ["COMMIT", "BUILD", "DEPLOY", "OBSERVE"], [{label:"OpenTelemetry documentation",url:"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/"}], [
      L("DevOps as a feedback loop","Beginner",["DevOps shares responsibility for delivering and operating software. It emphasizes automation, short feedback loops, dependable releases, and learning from incidents rather than passing work between disconnected teams.","It is not a vendor or a particular tool set. A small project can practice the core ideas with version control, a repeatable build, simple deployment, and useful logs.","Optimize for safe changes and recovery, not only speed. A release that cannot be monitored or rolled back can increase risk."],"A static site can publish reviewed changes and retain a prior deployment for rollback.","Name one repetitive release step to automate and a check that should run first.","What does a feedback loop help a team do?","Notice outcomes quickly and use evidence to improve the next change.","text","change -> checks -> release -> observe -> improve"),
      L("Git workflow and code review","Beginner",["Git stores project history. A branch isolates a change, a commit records a snapshot, and a pull request gives reviewers one focused place to inspect it.","Small commits make regressions easier to locate. Use meaningful messages and keep secrets, local output, and personal files out of the repository.","Review behavior, clarity, security, and release impact. Keep discussion respectful and explain the reason behind unfamiliar choices."],"A lesson update is reviewed as a small diff and merged after required checks pass.","Write a branch name and commit message for a new SQL path.","Why keep a commit focused?","It is easier to review, understand, and revert safely.","text","branch -> focused commit -> review -> merge"),
      L("Linux files, processes, and permissions","Beginner",["Linux exposes files, processes, users, and devices through a consistent filesystem model. Commands such as pwd, ls, and ps inspect the system; understand a command before using elevated privileges.","Permissions define who can read, write, or execute a file. Least privilege applies to user accounts, service processes, and deployed credentials alike.","Shell scripts help repeat tasks, but quoting and paths matter. Verify a target before running commands that recursively delete or overwrite files."],"A web service should use a limited account and only read its own protected configuration.","Explain why an internet-facing service should not run as an administrator.","Why avoid running a service as root?","A compromise would inherit broad permissions and could damage more of the system.","sh","pwd\nls -la\nps"),
      L("Containers and reproducible builds","Intermediate",["A container packages an application and dependencies into an image. It shares the host kernel, so it is not the same security boundary as a full virtual machine.","Use trusted base images, update them for fixes, and do not bake credentials into image layers. Deleting a secret later may leave it recoverable from an earlier layer.","Limit privileges, network access, writable paths, and resources. Containers improve consistency but do not make an application secure automatically."],"A preview can use a fixed runtime so local and hosted behavior match.","List two secrets or permissions that should stay outside a container image.","Why may deleting a secret in a later layer be insufficient?","Earlier image layers may still retain the value.","text","source + dependencies -> image -> container"),
      L("CI/CD and protected deployment","Intermediate",["Continuous integration runs repeatable checks when code changes. Delivery keeps a release ready to publish; continuous deployment publishes passing changes automatically.","A pipeline should fail clearly when a required check fails and protect credentials that can alter production. Build permissions and deployment permissions should be limited separately.","A green pipeline means only that its defined checks passed. Choose checks that represent important behavior and keep a recovery plan."],"A static-site pipeline checks required files, builds the site, and publishes from its main branch.","Choose and order three pipeline steps for an education website.","Why protect deployment credentials?","A leaked credential could alter the public site or connected services.","yaml","steps:\n  - name: Build\n    run: npm run build\n  - name: Publish\n    run: deploy-site"),
      L("Cloud responsibility, monitoring, and backup","Advanced",["Cloud platforms provide compute, storage, databases, and networks, but responsibility is shared. Customers still configure access and protect data. Choose components according to actual needs and operating capacity.","Logs describe events, metrics summarize measurements, and traces show a request across services. Record only useful details and avoid secrets or unnecessary personal data.","A backup is useful only when it can be restored. Define retention, access, and a recovery plan; monitor user-facing success, latency, errors, and resource limits."],"A lesson site can use static hosting for public content and a protected API only for account features.","Design one alert for a lesson API and a recovery step for a broken release.","Why practice restoring backups?","It confirms that data is complete and people can recover it under pressure.","text","detect -> contain -> restore -> learn")
    ])
  ];
  window.cyberShieldCurriculum = (window.cyberShieldCurriculum || []).concat(paths);
})();
